============================================================
SECTION A: MULTI-STEP DRIFT vs PER-STEP IDENTITY BASELINE
============================================================
The debate's Sec 3.4 diagnostic 1 requires the identity control reported
alongside EVERY drift number, not just horizon 1. At k=4 the frame stacks
s_t and s_{t+4} share zero overlapping frames (D1), so a persistently high
predictor similarity there is the strongest evidence available that the
model is not just exploiting frame-stack overlap.

  step 1 | predictor=0.9393  identity=0.9707  advantage=-0.0314  (shares 3/4 frames with input)
  step 2 | predictor=0.8936  identity=0.9504  advantage=-0.0567  (shares 2/4 frames with input)
  step 3 | predictor=0.8495  identity=0.9251  advantage=-0.0755  (shares 1/4 frames with input)
  step 4 | predictor=0.8264  identity=0.9178  advantage=-0.0914  (shares 0/4 frames with input) <-- zero frame overlap: overlap confound eliminated
  step 5 | predictor=0.8063  identity=0.9119  advantage=-0.1055  (shares 0/4 frames with input) <-- zero frame overlap: overlap confound eliminated

